			"id":"3369f33257f2f52e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Metaethics is ==the second-order branch of philosophy that investigates the nature, scope, origins, and meaning of moral judgments and values==. Unlike normative ethics (which asks \"What is right or wrong?\") or applied ethics (which asks \"Is this specific act right or wrong?\"), metaethics asks \"What is morality itself?\" [[1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metaethics), [2](https://www.reddit.com/r/philosophy/comments/1fvspw/an_introductionoverview_of_metaethics/), [3](https://iep.utm.edu/metaethi/), [4](https://study.com/academy/lesson/meta-ethics-definition-principles-real-life-applications.html), [5](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sETnOF5_ghg&t=1)]\n\nCore Questions of Metaethics\n\n- **Semantics:** What do moral terms like \"good,\" \"bad,\" \"right,\" and \"wrong\" actually mean? [[1](https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/religion-and-philosophy/metaethics/), [2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metaethics)]\n\n- **Ontology:** Do moral facts, properties, and truths exist independently of human minds (objective), or are they social or emotional constructs (subjective)? [[1](https://philpapers.org/browse/meta-ethics), [2](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GSuUQnGCFMs&t=11), [3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metaethics)]\n\n- **Epistemology:** How do human beings acquire moral knowledge, and how can moral beliefs be justified? [[1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metaethics), [2](https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/religion-and-philosophy/metaethics/)]\n\n- **Psychology:** What is the connection between making a moral judgment and being motivated to act on it? [[1](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaethics/), [2](https://study.com/academy/lesson/meta-ethics-definition-principles-real-life-applications.html), [3](https://www.reddit.com/r/philosophy/comments/1fvspw/an_introductionoverview_of_metaethics/)]\n\n---\n\nMain Theoretical Divides\n\n1. Cognitivism vs. Non-Cognitivism\n\n- **Cognitivism:** Asserts that moral statements express beliefs and are \"truth-apt\" (they can be either true or false). [[1](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JIT1W_mffG4), [2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metaethics)]\n\n- **Non-Cognitivism (Expressivism):** Asserts that moral statements do not express genuine beliefs, but rather emotional attitudes, commands, or prescriptions (e.g., saying \"Stealing is wrong\" is like saying \"Boo to stealing!\"). [[1](https://www.quora.com/What-is-meta-ethics-and-what-are-some-examples), [2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metaethics), [3](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JIT1W_mffG4)]\n\n2. Moral Realism vs. Anti-Realism\n\n- **Moral Realism:** Claims that objective moral facts exist in the universe independently of what any person or culture thinks. [[1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metaethics), [2](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JIT1W_mffG4), [3](https://www.quora.com/What-is-meta-ethics-and-what-are-some-examples)]\n\n- **Moral Anti-Realism:** Denies that objective moral facts exist. This splits into several sub-views:\n    - **Subjectivism:** Moral judgments are true or false relative to individual or cultural preferences.\n    - **Error Theory:** Claims that moral language tries to state objective facts, but all such statements are systematically false because objective values do not exist (defended by J.L. Mackie).\n    - **Nihilism:** Rejects the idea that anything has intrinsic moral value or meaning. [[1](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FOoffXFpAlU&t=3), [2](https://openstax.org/books/introduction-philosophy/pages/8-3-metaethics), [3](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GSuUQnGCFMs&t=11), [4](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metaethics), [5](https://www.quora.com/What-is-meta-ethics-and-what-are-some-examples), [6](https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/religion-and-philosophy/metaethics/)]\n\n3. Naturalism vs. Non-Naturalism\n\n- **Moral Naturalism:** Believes moral properties can be reduced to or identified with natural, empirical properties studied by science (such as pleasure, pain, or human flourishing). [[1](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GSuUQnGCFMs&t=11), [2](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaethics/), [3](https://openstax.org/books/introduction-philosophy/pages/8-3-metaethics)]\n\n- **Moral Non-Naturalism:** Believes moral properties are real and non-reducible to natural facts; they must be grasped through moral intuition or a unique non-empirical dimension (defended by G.E. Moore and ethical intuitionists). [[1](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaethics/), [2](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GSuUQnGCFMs&t=11)]\n\n---\n\nClassic Problems and Arguments\n\n- **Hume’s Is/Ought Gap:** David Hume argued that you cannot logically deduce what people _ought_ to do simply from a description of what _is_ the case in the physical world.\n\n- **Moore’s Open Question Argument:** G.E. Moore argued that any attempt to define moral \"goodness\" in natural terms (like \"pleasure\") always leaves an open question: \"Yes, it is pleasurable, but is it _good_?\"\n\n- **The Euthyphro Problem:** Asks whether something is right because God commands it, or if God commands it because it is independently right. [[1](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaethics/), [2](https://openstax.org/books/introduction-philosophy/pages/8-3-metaethics)]\n\nFor deeper academic overviews, you can explore the [Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaethics/) or the [Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy](https://iep.utm.edu/metaethi/). [[1](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaethics/), [2](https://iep.utm.edu/metaethi/)]\n\n",
